ZModem standard:
    libzmodem speaks standard ZMODEM.  The lrzsz options that left the
    standard (8k blocks with -8, timesync with -S) no longer exist.
    - The sender (zmodem_send, msz) uses subpackets of at most 1024
      bytes, a 32 bit CRC if the receiver announces it (16 bit
      otherwise), and escapes all control characters.  If the receiver
      does not announce control character escaping itself, the sender
      asks for it with a ZSINIT frame.
    - The sender does not request any conversion or management option
      and never sends commands (ZCOMMAND).
    - The receiver (zmodem_receive, mrz) accepts subpackets of up to
      8192 bytes with either CRC, and ignores ZCOMMAND frames: remote
      command execution has been removed.

XModem and YModem:
    are not supported.  Some of the YMODEM fallback code of lrzsz is
    still present (the receiver starts asking for a YMODEM batch if no
    ZMODEM sender answers), but it is not tested and not part of the
    API.

Calling conventions:
    libzmodem is not compatible with rz/sz or lrz/lsz on the command
    line: mrz and msz are small sample programs with their own options
    (mrz: -b BPS, -d DIR, -q; msz: -b BPS, -q), and both always talk
    on standard input and standard output.  They exit with status 0 if
    the session succeeded and 1 if it failed.

    The receiver differs from older UNIX ZModems in these ways:
    - it always runs in what lrz called restricted mode.  A file name
      containing "../", or one that would overwrite an existing file
      whose name starts with a dot, ends the session.
    - a file with an absolute pathname is only accepted if the
      approver callback accepts it; without an approver callback it is
      skipped.  mrz accepts every name it is offered.
    - PUBDIR (/var/spool/uucppublic) is normally disabled. If you like
      public writable directories please use:
        configure --enable-pubdir=/var/spool/uucppublic
      An absolute pathname outside of PUBDIR then ends the session,
      even if the approver callback accepted it.
    - a file that already exists is skipped, unless the sender asks
      for something else with a ZMODEM management option (overwrite,
      append, rename, newer, different CRC) or asks to resume.  msz
      never does.
    - received files get the modification time and the permission
      bits the sender reports for them.
    - if a session fails, the file that was being received is removed.

Timeouts:
    Without any answer from the other side the sender gives up after
    about 30 seconds and the receiver after about 160 seconds.
